@startuml Who you are - the signed anonymous cookie
title Who you are: the signed anonymous cookie\nThere is no sign-in. One HttpOnly cookie, signed by the server, is the whole identity.

skinparam shadowing false
skinparam sequenceMessageAlign center
skinparam responseMessageBelowArrow true

actor Visitor as V
participant "Browser" as B
participant "Root layout\n(server component)" as L
participant "POST /api/session" as S
participant "Any /api/... route" as A
database "MongoDB" as DB

== First visit: the cookie is minted ==

V -> B : open the app
B -> L : request the page
L -> L : read the v2v_uid cookie\n-> missing, forged or over 30 days old
L --> B : the page, plus a small client component\nwhose only job is the call below
B -> S : POST /api/session (no body)
S -> S : mint a random UUID and sign it:\nv2v_uid = <uuid>.<issuedAt>.<HMAC-SHA256 of the two,\nkeyed with SESSION_COOKIE_SECRET>
S --> B : 204 No Content\nSet-Cookie: v2v_uid=...; HttpOnly; Secure;\nSameSite=Lax; Path=/; Max-Age=1 year
note over B
  HttpOnly, so no script on the page can read it.
  The signature is what makes a user id unforgeable:
  the secret never leaves the server.
end note

== Every request after that ==

B -> A : the browser attaches the cookie by itself
A -> A : split it, then compare the signature\nin constant time

alt the signature checks out
  A -> DB : every query filtered by { userId }
  DB --> A : this visitor's rows and no one else's
  A --> B : the response
  opt the cookie is more than 30 days old
    A --> B : Set-Cookie: the same user id, freshly signed\n(a returning visitor never loses their history)
  end
else missing, tampered with, or malformed
  A -> A : treated exactly like a first visit\n-> a brand-new user id
  A --> B : the response + Set-Cookie for the new id
  note right of A
    Nothing is "rejected": an unreadable cookie
    simply means a new anonymous visitor, who
    sees an empty history.
  end note
end

note across
  The trade-off this buys: no passwords, no accounts, no personal data - but clearing
  cookies, or switching browser or device, means starting over with an empty history.
end note
@enduml
